 ```
 
-Password storage and token comparison live in `accounts.crypto`, which the reset
-flow calls rather than hashing or comparing anything itself. Issuing a token and
-checking one are implemented; completing a reset is not.
+## Reset flow
+
+1. `request_reset(email)` issues a random token (256 bits) and records its issue time.
+   Deliver it to the account owner out of band; it is never logged. Several tokens may be
+   outstanding for one account.
+2. `complete_reset(email, token, new_password)` returns `True` and stores the new password
+   only if the token was issued for that account, has not been used, and is under 30 minutes
+   old. Otherwise it returns `False` and changes nothing; it does not raise.
+3. On success every outstanding token for the account is cleared, so a token is single use
+   and any other pending reset links stop working. Expired tokens are discarded when checked.
+
+```python
+from accounts.reset import complete_reset, request_reset
+
+token = request_reset("user@example.com")
+assert complete_reset("user@example.com", token, "new password")
+assert not complete_reset("user@example.com", token, "again")  # single use
+```
+
+Passwords are stored as salted scrypt hashes and tokens are compared in constant time, both in
+`accounts.crypto`; the reset flow calls these rather than hashing or comparing anything itself.
+State is held in memory (`USERS`, `RESET_TOKENS`).
